Branching and Merging –

echo “Hello from main” > app.txt
git status (checks where file is will be red if its not added)
git add app.txt 
git status (should be green)
git commit -m “initial commit on main”

git checkout -b branch-1 (creates a new branch)
echo “Feature branch change” > app.txt
git add app.txt (adding the file to another branch)
git commit -m “update from branch-1” (sends a commit from branch-1)

git checkout main
echo “Main branch change” > app.txt
git add app.txt
git commit -m “conflicting update from main”

git merge branch-1
echo -e “Hello from main\nMain branch change\nFeature branch change” > app.txt
cat app.txt (displays the text)
git add app.txt
git commit -m “resolve merge conflict between main and branch-1 branch”
git push (fixes merge conflicts)

git branch -d branch-1 (deletes the branch we don’t want to make it look clean on github)
git branch (shows the branches)

Git Workflow –

How to use git as a team –

git pull (make sure you have the latest things)
git checkout -b feature/add-about-page (check out of your current branch and create a new one)
echo “This is the about page.” > about.md
git status
git add about.md
git commit -m “Add about page”
git push 
git push –set-upstream origin feature/add-about-page (adds it remotely to github)

Github will tell you on the site there’s a new branch added and will ask if you want to do a pull request, so just click it and create pull request.
You can then get someone to review it
And then eventually merge pull request

Undoing in Git –

git checkout main (checks out of current branch and goes to main)
git pull (gets the latest things)

Example 1 – How to undo something you haven’t pushed yet
echo “origin line” > undo.txt
git add undo.txt
git commit -m “test commit” 
echo “bad change” > undo.txt
cat undo.txt (will display “bad change”)
git restore undo.txt
cat undo.txt (will display “origin line”)

Example 2 – How to unstage 

echo “another bad line” > undo.txt
git add undo.txt
git status
git restore –staged undo.txt
git status (now unstaged)

Example 3 – How to undo a bad commit that hasn’t been pushed

echo “oops commit” > undo.txt
git status
git add undo.txt
git commit -m “bad commit”
git reset –soft HEAD~1
git status

Git Stash –

git status
git pull
echo “incomplete work” > feature.txt
git add feature.txt
echo “more changes not staged” > feature.txt
git stash push -m “WIP: feature.txt changes”
git status
echo “hotfix applied” > hotfix.txt
git add hotfix.txt
git commit -m “apply hotfix on main”
git push origin main
git stash list

To bring back work:
git stash apply
git stash pop
git stash list (it wont be there anymore)

Git rebase & squash –

git pull 
git status
git checkout -b feature-rebase
echo “line 1” > changes.txt
git add changes.txt
git commit -m “feat: add first line”
echo “line 2” >> changes.txt
git status
git add changes.txt
git commit -m “feat: add second line”
echo “line 3” >> changes.txt
git add changes.txt
git commit -m “feat: add third line”
git log –oneline

we want to rebase and squash all these 3 commits into one:

git rebase -i HEAD~3
Then change the “pick” in the second and third line to “squash”
And then save and close with wq!
git log –oneline
git push
will display as one commit on github

Git cherry-pick –

git pull
git checkout -b feature-cherry
echo “hotfix config for prod” > hotfix.txt
git add hotfix.txt
git commit -m “hotfix: add prod config fix”
git push
git log –oneline (copy the commit number on the left)
git checkout main
git pull
git cherry-pick 57a34bc (cherry picks a commit from one branch to the main)
git push origin main

